{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-04-exponential-equations-factoring",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Exponential Equations by Factoring",
    "description": "Factor out the smallest power so sums and differences of exponentials become simple common-base equations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Make the key misconception visible first: exponents do not add across a plus sign. Instead, factor the smallest common power, reduce the bracket to a small ordinary number, and only then compare powers.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the chapter pattern exemplified by 2^(x−1)+2^(x+1): factor the smaller power, simplify the small bracket, then convert the remaining value to the same base. Cover adjacent and non-adjacent powers, sums and differences, shifted exponents such as n−1 and n+1, expressions whose common-power ratio becomes a constant, and squared-exponent forms such as 3^(2x)+3^(2x+1). Stress that terms may not be combined by adding exponents; exponents add only when powers are multiplied. Finish with a capstone that finds x first and then reports a requested expression in x."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The fastest way to solve a sum like 2^(x−1)+2^(x+1) is not to guess x.\n\nThe two terms share a large common piece. Pull that piece out, and the intimidating equation becomes a tiny multiplication problem.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Take 2^(x−1)+2^(x+1)=1280.\n\nThe smaller power is 2^(x−1). The second term is four times that power.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Factor it:\n\n2^(x−1)(1+2^2)=1280\n5×2^(x−1)=1280\n2^(x−1)=256=2^8\nSo x=9.\n\nThe plus sign stayed outside the exponent law; factoring handled it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
